{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310755,"nonce":"vS4hN8UdL2pjAKhRRL6OdRX/ZLg=","title":"Detailed hand-off: the 2 wasm Thread.spawn-worker bugs (WorkerPool fanout traps on host imports — CONFIRMED root; cross-thread Channel ...","message":"Hand-off analysis for the two remaining wasm concurrency bugs on branch `wasm-default-inguest-matmul`. They look related but have **DIFFERENT roots** (proven below).\n\n- `rayzor_thread_spawn` (wasm host fn, ~3901): lazily builds a persistent `WorkerPool` of N OS threads (N = available_parallelism clamped 2..8) on first spawn (3917-3932); then dispatches the closure as a `Job{fn_idx, env_ptr, slot}` to the next worker round-robin via an **mpsc channel** (3947-3960), returns a thread id. The closure runs CONCURRENTLY on the worker.\n- `worker_main` (~1155): each worker has its OWN wasmtime `Store`+`Instance` over the ONE shared `SharedMemory`, reserves a private 1 MiB shadow stack (serialized under RESERVE_LOCK — the flash-race fix [[bugs_wasm_flash_parallel_sharedmem_race]]), then loops `for job in rx`: looks up `fn_idx` in `__indirect_function_table` and `call`s it.\n- **THE KEY CONSTRAINT (line 1171):** `linker.define_unknown_imports_as_traps(\u0026module)` — the worker linker defines ONLY `env.memory`; EVERY host import is stubbed to a TRAP. Workers were built strictly for PURE-COMPUTE closures (matmul row-bands, flash q-head bands) that call only IN-GUEST merged functions. In-guest fns (rayzor_arc_*, rayzor_mutex_*, rayzor_channel_* — all in runtime-wasm/src/concurrent.rs) are INTERNAL to the merged module, so workers CAN call them. But any closure that calls a HOST import traps.\n- `worker_main` previously SWALLOWED the trap (`.call(...).unwrap_or(0)`) → a trapping closure silently \"completes\" with 0. **FIXED this session: it now `eprintln!`s `[wasm-worker N] closure TRAPPED: {e}`** (loud, per the original 1168-1170 intent). matmul/flash workers don't trap, so no change for them.\n- The matmul/flash pattern: main dispatches N band jobs then JOINS them (`join_worker` blocks the main thread on the slot Condvar) → main idle, workers run, publish, main wakes. **A fire-and-forget spawn (no join) + a busy main is a DIFFERENT scheduling regime** — see Bug 2.\n\nThe original \"WorkerPool hangs/traps\" turned out to be three separate things. Investigated + 2 fixed (commit 25d9d7d4):\n- **(a) wasm worker traps on host imports — partly inherent.** The worker linker stubs every host import to a trap (wasm_runner.rs:1171). The fanout closure called `CpuTopology.bindToNode` (a host fn) → trap on the worker (surfaced by the loud-trap diagnostic, commit 24e0c157). FIXED for bindToNode: implemented rayzor_topology_* IN-GUEST in runtime-wasm (no-op single-node defaults → merged, callable on a worker, no trap). BUT a worker closure that calls ANY other host import still traps — e.g. `Sys.println`/`trace` inside a worker closure traps (so A1.hx, which prints in the worker, is a BAD repro; use A2.hx which only computes + Arc\u003cMutex\u003e). Inherent: workers can't do host I/O.\n- **(b) native bindToNode-in-4-threads SIGILL (exit 132).** A W0020-class trap-stub strand on `rayzor_topology_bind_to_node`'s CALL, deterministic for that IR (cpuToNode in 4 threads is FINE, bindToNode in 2 threads is FINE — only bindToNode×4). Making the native bind impl a no-op did NOT fix it (it's the call, not the body). FIXED by guarding bindToNode behind `multiNode()` in WorkerPool.hx so it's never called on UMA (macOS/wasm/laptop). Repro /tmp/par/MP.hx (132) vs MP3 (no bind, 0).\n- **(c) THE REAL REMAINING BLOCKER — native WorkerPool crash exit 139 (SIGSEGV).** Distinct from (b)'s SIGILL. After the multiNode guard, WP1/WP2/A2 STILL SIGSEGV. Bisected to: the worker thread closure captures the `fn` closure PARAMETER of parallelFor/parallelRows and calling `f(lo,hi,node)` segfaults. A hand-written equivalent with a LOCAL closure works (MP7/MP8: capture a local 3-arg closure + lo/hi/node, call it in a 4-thread loop → sum correct). So the trigger is capturing a closure-typed METHOD PARAMETER (`var f = fn`) into a Thread.spawn closure, NOT loop-capture or arg count. Same family as the closure-capture / heap-marshaling work. **NEXT: reproduce minimally** — a top-level fn `run(fn:(Int)-\u003eVoid)` that does `Thread.spawn(() -\u003e { fn(0); })` — does capturing the param `fn` into the thread closure segfault? If so, it's closure-parameter capture across Thread.spawn (likely the env for `fn` isn't transferred / `fn`'s value is the param slot which is stale on the worker thread). Repro /tmp/par/WP1.hx, /tmp/par/A2.hx.\n**VERIFY (when fixed):** A2.hx (parallelRows into Arc\u003cMutex\u003e) PASS native + wasm.\n\n**Symptom:** producer Thread.spawn worker sends 1000 to a cap-4 channel while main receives → ~1-2/8 PASS, rest 30s timeout. Repro /tmp/par/chx.hx. Native 5/5.\n**RULED OUT:** (a) channel logic — native 5/5, single-thread wasm 5/5; (b) worker trap — NO `TRAPPED` line for chx (the producer is all in-guest: arc_get + channel_send); (c) futex-park reliability per se — spin (no park) → 6/6 deadlock (WORSE), infinite futex → 2/5, timed futex → 1/8. The pattern across (c): the producer worker isn't making PROGRESS, and a busy/spinning main makes it worse.\n**LEADING HYPOTHESES (untested — experiments below):**\n- H1 (scheduling): the worker runs concurrently but the busy main starves it, OR wasmtime needs the main to BLOCK (like the matmul join) for the worker's Store to get CPU/epoch time. Note the only proven-good worker pattern is dispatch-then-JOIN (main blocks). A producer that runs while main is busy-looping on the channel is novel.\n- H2 (cross-instance visibility under sustained contention): channel SLOT writes are NON-ATOMIC (store_word); head/tail are atomic (seqcst). The flash-race noted wasmtime SharedMemory cross-instance non-atomic-write visibility quirks (later attributed to shadow-stack, but the channel hammers it harder). A stale slot/head/tail read could stall the protocol.\n**EXPERIMENTS (cheapest first):**\n1. Does the worker make progress at all when main does NOT contend? Spawn producer (cap 10, 5 sends, never blocks), main `Thread.sleep(400)`, then drain via tryReceive. (Fix /tmp/par/chd.hx — `.length()` was wrong; use the right method or tryReceive.) If it drains 5 → worker runs, issue is the blocking/contention interplay (H1 contention). If 0 → worker doesn't run unjoined (H1 dispatch).\n2. Make the channel SLOTS atomic (i32 atomic load/store instead of store_word/load_word) — rules H2 in/out cheaply (rebuild runtime-wasm only).\n3. Have the consumer YIELD instead of park: between receive attempts call a guest yield (or a longer futex timeout) to give the worker more CPU; measure pass rate.\n4. If H1-dispatch: check whether an unjoined worker's Store gets scheduled — instrument worker_main to print when it picks up the job + each Nth send. (The producer SHOULD print steady progress if running.)\n**Likely fix direction:** if H1, the channel's blocking should cooperate with the worker pool (e.g. the parked side must yield CPU in a way wasmtime schedules the worker; a host-side condvar like join_worker uses, rather than a guest futex, may be required — but that's \"out of the wasm model\" per the flash-race notes). If H2, atomic slots + a release/acquire fence.\n\n`worker_main` surfaces worker-closure traps loudly (`[wasm-worker N] closure TRAPPED: {e}`) instead of swallowing them — this is what pinned Bug 1 and will catch any future worker-trap regression. Keep it.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_worker_scheduling_analysis` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}